        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveroutdoors.com/history/history-of-lake-wylie/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Catawba River was named for the Native American tribe who first lived in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lakewyliecarolinas.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The man-made lake first formed in 1904 when the Catawba Power Company built the Catawba Dam and Power Plant.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lakewyliecarolinas.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Buster Boyd Bridge has connected North and South Carolina across the lake since 1923.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://lakewyliecarolinas.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Recent efforts to resurvey the state line using GPS technology moved the border approximately 150 feet southward in Lake Wylie.",
